

Important prerequisite problem...

Given n <= 3e5, w <= 2n, and ai bi <= 1e9, which are costs to take values 1 and 2, with ai < bi (1 <= i <= n); find minimum cost to achieve w. 

Consider the naive and incorrect greedy: take the option of highest value per cost each time. This might fail when we take both 1 and 2...

Consider transforming the objects: we can take ai, and then possibly take bi-ai if ai is already taken. We order all of these objects by their value  descendingly, and take greedily the largest. The issue now is we might take bi-ai without taking ai...

Case 1) ai < bi-ai, or 2ai < bi, then one would never take bi-ai first in the greedy, these aibi are fine, we do (ai, 1), (bi-ai, 1) as usual

Case 2) ai >= bi-ai, or 2ai >= bi, in this case we may have taken bi-ai first, which is problematic; however, we have the intuition that taking bi is generally better... we first disallow taking only a by forcing only one object of weight bi with value 2: (bi, 2)!

These objects constitute set X. Suppose we took set K.

Claim. K has at most one Case 2) where that choice of disallowing was actually wrong.

Proof. Suppose taking two Case 2) where that choice of disallowing was wrong is uniquely optimal.

ai aj
aj bj

with 2ai >= bi and 2aj >= bj. Suppose ai <= aj, then bi <= 2ai <= ai+aj, meaning we can replace ai, aj with (bi, 2) to obtain an at least better arrangement. Contradiction.

We take greedily until full or exceed by 1.

If full, what we took must have been perfect...

If full+1, which means we last took a 2
- if that 2 is retained, we find the costliest to remove
- if that 2 is discarded, we find the cheapest to add
Both are O(n)

Note that the Case 2) wrong choice is considered in full+1 and cannot have happened in full.









